    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Dwale sits in north‑central Floyd County on the west side of the Levisa Fork, just north of Allen.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dwale%2C_Kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Dwale was never a large town or mining city; instead, it remained a quiet mountain community shaped by water, roads, kinship, and work.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        18,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://appalachianhistorian.org/dwale-floyd-county-haws-fork-hawes-ford-and-a-community-name-that-remained/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In the mountains, settlement followed stream valleys, and families built where bottom land allowed houses, gardens, stores, roads, churches, schools, and crossings.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://appalachianhistorian.org/dwale-floyd-county-haws-fork-hawes-ford-and-a-community-name-that-remained/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Levisa Fork was a path through Floyd County, and along it small communities like Dwale grew, often first appearing in postal records before books.",
